Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book by Margery Williams
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Bright morning sunshine streams into a cozy, carpeted nursery decorated with green holly sprigs. In the top of a red hanging stocking sits VELVETEEN RABBIT—a fat, plush toy rabbit with spotted brown and white fur, real thread whiskers, and pink sateen ears.

(SOUND: muffled festive chatter from downstairs, steady ticking mantel clock)

THE BOY (7, bright-eyed, wearing flannel pajamas) rushes to the mantelpiece and pulls Velveteen Rabbit from the stocking, clutching him in a warm, tight hug.

THE BOY
You're the best present of all.

NANA (50s, crisp nursemaid in a white apron) enters carrying a stack of unwrapped presents wrapped in rustling tissue paper.

(SOUND: rustling paper, clattering wooden toys)

NANA
Come along now! Uncles and aunts are waiting downstairs with more parcels!

The Boy sets Velveteen Rabbit down on the carpet near a model boat and a clockwork mouse, running out the doorway after Nana.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight pours through the nursery window, casting long shadows across the quiet room. Velveteen Rabbit rests against the fender, his pink sateen ears drooping softly. Beside him stands SKIN HORSE—a tall, weathered toy horse with bald patches in his brown coat and exposed seams.

(SOUND: soft night wind against the glass, steady ticking clock)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real, a great many years ago. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

Velveteen Rabbit sighs softly, resting his plush head against Skin Horse's wooden leg.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Hours later, the nursery is dark save for a dim night-light burning on the mantelpiece. Nana swoops into the room carrying an oil lamp, searching the carpet floorboards.

(SOUND: creaking floorboards, rustling bedsheets)

NANA
Where has that china dog gotten to? It's far too late for hunting toys.

Nana spots Velveteen Rabbit sitting near the open toy cupboard door, grabs him by one pink ear, and carries him over to the Boy's bed.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana tucks Velveteen Rabbit into the bed. The Boy immediately wraps his warm arms around the plush rabbit, snuggling him close under his chin.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks later. Soft amber light glows from the night-light. Velveteen Rabbit lies tucked under a blanket tunnel constructed by the Boy. His brown spots are slightly faded and the pink velvet on his nose is worn smooth.

(SOUND: gentle rhythmic breathing of sleeping child, distant crickets outside)

THE BOY
These are the deep burrows where the real rabbits live. You'll stay right here with me all night.

The Boy pulls Velveteen Rabbit tightly under his chin, drifting off to sleep.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Sunlight illuminates a lush lawn fringed with red raspberry canes and flower borders. Velveteen Rabbit sits in a small wooden wheelbarrow. His plush coat is noticeably worn, his tail stitching unsewn, and his whiskers thin.

(SOUND: buzzing bumblebees, birds chirping in trees)

The Boy pushes the wheelbarrow through the grass before diving into a dirt hollow near the raspberry bushes to play.

Nana walks across the lawn toward them, shaking her apron in mild frustration.

NANA
Look at the state of him! Wet with dew and covered in dirt from those flower beds. Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Boy clutches Velveteen Rabbit tightly to his chest. Velveteen Rabbit's worn boot-button eyes shine with quiet joy.

EXT. WOODED GLADE - EVENING

Golden dusk filters through high oak branches onto a patch of tall bracken fern. Velveteen Rabbit rests comfortably in a nest of soft fern leaves, his shabby fur dusted with pollen.

(SOUND: evening rustle of leaves, distant wood-pigeon calls)

A line of tiny ants scurries across Velveteen Rabbit's velvet paws. Suddenly, two wild rabbits—WILD RABBIT ONE and WILD RABBIT TWO, both with sleek brown fur, twitching noses, and nimble hind legs—creep out from the tall bracken.

Wild Rabbit One steps close, twitching his pink nose continuously.

(SOUND: soft rustle of dry fern)

WILD RABBIT ONE
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT ONE
It's as easy as anything!

Wild Rabbit One leaps sideways into the air, landing lightly on his hind legs and whirling in a circle.

WILD RABBIT ONE
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT ONE
Can you hop on your hind legs?

Velveteen Rabbit sits motionless in the bracken, tucking his single-piece plush lower body under his belly.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

Wild Rabbit One leans forward, brushing his long whiskers right against Velveteen Rabbit's ear. Suddenly, Wild Rabbit One flattens his ears and jumps backward with a start.

WILD RABBIT ONE
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

(SOUND: heavy crunching footsteps approaching through dry leaves)

The Boy runs down the dirt path nearby. With a flash of white tails, both wild rabbits bound off into the dark wood.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

The forest grows quiet. Velveteen Rabbit lies alone in the darkening fern, watching the empty trees.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Pale afternoon light filters through closed window drapes. The Boy lies flushed and feverish in bed, his forehead beaded with sweat. Velveteen Rabbit sits wedged under the bedclothes against the Boy's burning chest—his whiskers completely gone, his ear linings grey, his brown spots faded to faint patches.

(SOUND: heavy fevered breathing, ticking clock)

DOCTOR (60s, carrying a black leather medical bag) places a hand on the Boy's forehead and smiles gently at Nana.

DOCTOR
The fever has broken at last. He will recover nicely now.

NANA
Thank heavens. I'll get him packed for the seaside tomorrow.

DOCTOR
Mind you, this entire room must be disinfected. Every book and toy he touched in bed must be burnt.

Nana reaches into the sheets and pulls out Velveteen Rabbit by his worn neck.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more.

EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

Behind the wooden fowl-house, a pile of old picture books and paper waste fills an open burlap sack resting against the garden wall. Moonlight shines on Velveteen Rabbit as he wriggles his shabby head through the neck of the sack, looking out at the distant garden.

(SOUND: cold night wind whistling through fence boards)

Velveteen Rabbit gazes toward the dark raspberry canes where he once played. His worn coat provides no warmth against the chill air.

(MUSIC: solemn cello theme rising softly)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ends like this?

A single, clear tear forms in Velveteen Rabbit's boot-button eye, trickles down his shabby velvet nose, and drops onto the soil below.

(SOUND: soft, magical shimmering tone ringing through the air)

Where the tear fell, a slender green stem shoots up out of the earth. Emerald leaves unfurl, opening into a golden cup-shaped blossom.

Out of the golden blossom steps NURSERY FAIRY—an ethereal figure clad in a shimmering gown of dew-drops and pearl, with wild flowers in her hair.

NURSERY FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I think I have seen your face before.

NURSERY FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

Nursery Fairy gathers Velveteen Rabbit up into her arms and kisses his damp velvet nose.

EXT. FOREST GLADE - NIGHT

Silver moonlight illuminates a grassy clearing framed by tall pine trees and frosted fern fronds. A dozen wild rabbits circle together on the soft turf.

(SOUND: soft rhythmic thumping of rabbit feet, distant owl call)

Nursery Fairy floats down from the sky, holding Velveteen Rabbit in her arms, and sets him gently onto the velvet grass.

NURSERY FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

(MUSIC: gentle woodwind melody rising softly)

Nursery Fairy kisses Velveteen Rabbit once more and steps back.

Velveteen Rabbit sits motionless on the grass, staring shyly at the dancing wild rabbits, afraid to reveal his rigid body.

(SOUND: subtle rustle of grass)

A stray blade of grass tickles Velveteen Rabbit's nose. Automatically, he raises his hind leg to scratch it.

He freezes in wonder. Beneath him stretch two long, muscular hind legs covered in soft, glossy brown fur. His ears twitch freely in the breeze, and long whiskers sweep the grass.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
My legs... I have hind legs!

(SOUND: energetic thumping and rustling of leaping rabbits)

Velveteen Rabbit leaps high into the air with a joyful bound, spinning sideways and dancing across the soft turf alongside the wild rabbits. When he turns back to look, Nursery Fairy has vanished into the moonlight.

EXT. WOODED GLADE - DAY

Months later. Warm spring sunlight floods the green glade, dappling the carpet of bracken and wildflowers. The Boy (8, now healthy and vibrant, wearing a dark wool jacket) walks along the forest path, searching the trees.

(SOUND: cheerful birdsong, rustling forest canopy)

From the tall bracken, two wild rabbits creep out to watch him. One rabbit is sleek brown; the other is brown with distinct faint markings under his fur, reminiscent of old brown and white spots, with familiar round black eyes and a gentle soft nose.

The Boy halts on the path, staring straight at the spotted rabbit.

THE BOY
Why... he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted REAL VELVETEEN RABBIT twitches his nose, gives a mighty leap into the air, and bounds away through the sunny ferns into the deep forest.

(SOUND: rustling bracken fading into peaceful woodland silence)

FADE OUT.

THE END
